            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>In overseas ad buying, three “invisible failures” eat the most time: account restrictions, rejected creatives, and top-ups that never land. They rarely surface before the damage — usually everything breaks at once when a campaign stops or settlement won't reconcile. The six questions below come from real day-to-day operations in overseas ad teams, and each answer includes a clear verdict and a concrete action, so you can convert them straight into an audit SOP.</p>
<h2>1. Account and creative issues</h2>
<p><strong>Q1: An ad account is suddenly restricted or suspended — what is the first step?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: Don't top up blindly, and don't rush to spin up replacement accounts through another agency. First, collect evidence: screenshot the restriction notice, note the affected ad IDs and the last normal delivery time. Second, classify the incident: quota/limit (insufficient balance, payment method flagged), content review (a single creative rejected), or risk control (the entire account is flagged). Each path requires a different response.</p>
<p>Recommended sequence: screenshot → check balance and payment method → review recent changes (a new landing-page domain? new targeting? an updated business entity?) → submit one concise appeal stating business legitimacy, paying entity, and campaign purpose → simultaneously check whether other accounts under the same entity were affected. Most “unexplained” restrictions trace back to one overlooked change, such as a newly added low-reputation redirect domain.</p>
<p><strong>Q2: Creative rejection rate jumps from 20% to 60% — how do you find the root cause fast?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: A rising rejection rate is rarely about one creative. It is at least one of three signals: creative template, landing page, or audience. The three most common triggers are:</p>
<ol>
<li>Over-promising copy (“100% effective,” “guaranteed returns,” “the only official…”);</li>
<li>Front-end/back-end mismatch (the video promotes an overseas brand while the landing page uses a domestic domain or Chinese-language support);</li>
<li>Batch reuse of the same body creative and copy, submitted repeatedly in a short window.</li>
</ol>
<p>Actions: cluster the last seven days of rejected creatives by copy keywords and find recurring sensitive terms; randomly pull three rejected landing pages and check domain registration age, redirect depth, and whether they push aggressive download/subscribe prompts; then compare targeting between rejected and approved creatives. If the pattern points to similarity, change the first frame and voiceover; if it points to copy, rewrite and strip absolute claims. A productivity app resolved this on the same day after the three checks — the root cause was a landing page moved to a newly registered domain.</p>
<p><strong>Q3: We run a dozen-plus ad accounts under one entity — how do we avoid being flagged for abusive duplication?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: Multiple accounts are not a violation. What triggers risk control is technically linkable signals — login environment, paying entity, creative fingerprints, and redirect domain overlap — more than account count itself.</p>
<p>Actions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Group accounts: 3–5 groups split by business line, country, and budget, with 1–3 accounts each;</li>
<li>Isolate environments: different device and network per group; never log into multiple accounts from one browser session;</li>
<li>Isolate payments: prefer different payment methods and email domains per group;</li>
<li>Differentiate creatives: avoid reusing identical video and copy within a group; vary first frame and voiceover;</li>
<li>Stagger operations: don't submit budgets or edit targeting across accounts in the same second.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Money and reconciliation</h2>
<p><strong>Q4: Top-ups arrive late and balances don't match spend — where do we start reconciling?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: First distinguish “delayed arrival” from “mismatch.” Pull two datasets and cross-check them: available balance and spend curves from the ad platform, and top-up statements from the payment channel (including fees, FX, refunds). Gaps usually come from three sources: FX and fees, top-ups still in transit, and refunds or failed charges silently deducted.</p>
<p>Actions: build a minimal reconciliation table with top-up order number, arrival time, currency, FX rate, fee, backend balance snapshot, reason for difference, and status. Fix a weekly snapshot slot (Monday morning works well) to export balances and spend, then reconcile line by line against top-up statements. Don't write off differences as “a platform issue” before checking the order status with the payment provider — it may be a failed charge clawback or an in-flight transfer. A cross-border e-commerce team moved reconciliation from “end of month” to “Monday morning checks” and caught two duplicate auto-debits in the same month, avoiding a drained balance and a campaign stoppage.</p>
<p><strong>Q5: Is agency-assisted top-up, rebate, and cashback shared in a chat group compliant?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: The compliance risk sits in two things: an opaque money trail that makes business legitimacy impossible to prove, and top-ups through third parties that violate most payment-provider and platform user agreements and can trigger account review. The test is simple: where did the money come from, can you invoice it, is it traceable in the ad platform, and is any third party holding the entity? Actions: use only traceable channels and keep order records; structure rebates through a contract or a proper service invoice rather than group red packets or personal transfers; avoid one payment account topping up for multiple advertisers. Keep a “top-up — spend — entity” mapping table between finance and the buying team so audits and appeals are one click away.</p>
<h2>3. Locking the audit into an SOP</h2>
<p><strong>Q6: What should the audit cover, how often, and who owns escalation?</strong></p>
<p>Verdict: Auditing isn't staring at every system daily — it's layered. Daily layer (15 minutes): account status and balance. Weekly layer: rejection rate, CPI and spend volatility, creative reuse, top-up reconciliation. Monthly layer: whether entity documentation has expired (business license, authorization letter, domain records, privacy policy).</p>
<p>A weekly checklist you can use as is:</p>
<ul>
<li>Account status: count of normal/restricted/suspended accounts, and whether each restriction has a ticket;</li>
<li>Balance: accounts below the safety line (e.g., 3× average daily spend);</li>
<li>Creatives: rejection rate, keyword clustering of rejected copy, count of duplicate submissions;</li>
<li>Reconciliation: this week's matched top-ups and the difference list;</li>
<li>Multi-account: whether new accounts overlap existing ones on environment, payment, or creative;</li>
<li>Documentation: validity of entity papers, landing-page domains, and privacy policy.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write the escalation path into the document: balance below the safety line → notify the buying owner same day; a single account restricted → submit appeal within 2 hours and inform the client; two or more accounts under one entity restricted simultaneously → escalate immediately to the compliance owner and pause all top-ups for that entity. A mobile game publisher that defined its safety line as 3× average daily spend has not had a balance-driven delivery stop since.</p>
<p>One closing note: the real value of auditing isn't recording “all good.” It's having the evidence chain prepared in advance. Screenshots, order numbers, creative versions, entity documents — these are the only things that help a client resume delivery quickly when an account is restricted.</p>
